@meta
id: 27-11
title: Why is my prose wrapping
chapter: 27
type: lesson
skills: verbose-set, autocmd
par: 118
minutes: 7
vscode: nothing — word wrap in VS Code is a view setting, not an editing one
open: CHANGELOG.md
vimrc: source config.vim|e

@brief
Add one line to the `Fixed` section of the changelog:

    - Refunds outside the 30 day window now record a reason instead of failing.

Type it and vim will break it in half somewhere around the word "record".
Not wrapped on screen — actually broken, with a newline written into the
file.

That is `textwidth`, and something set it to 60 for this buffer. Find out
what, turn it off for this buffer, and get the line in as one line.

`config.vim` is in this directory and is not yours to change.

@keys
| `:verbose setlocal textwidth?` | the value, and who set it |
| `:setlocal textwidth=0` | 0 means never break lines while typing |
| `:setlocal fo-=t` | keep textwidth, stop it applying to plain text |
| `:verbose autocmd FileType` | list FileType autocommands and their sources |

@hints
The line is being broken as you type it, which is a formatting option and
not a display one. Ask vim who set `textwidth` before you guess a fix.
--
`:verbose setlocal textwidth?` names the file and the line. Then
`:setlocal textwidth=0`.
--
`:setlocal textwidth=0` `Enter`, then `G`, `o`, type the line, `Esc`,
`:wq` `Enter`.

@solution
    :setlocal textwidth=0<Enter>
    Go- Refunds outside the 30 day window now record a reason instead of failing.<Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

A hundred and four keystrokes, seventy-five of them the sentence.

Two things showed up here for the first time.

**`:verbose` on a local option.** `:verbose set tw?` and
`:verbose setlocal tw?` can give different answers, because `set` reports
whichever value applies and `setlocal` reports the buffer's own. When you
are chasing a per-file surprise, ask with `setlocal`.

**The answer was an autocommand.** `config.vim` does not contain
`set textwidth=60` anywhere. It contains this:

    autocmd FileType markdown setlocal textwidth=60

An autocommand is a standing instruction: *when this event happens to a
buffer matching this pattern, run this command*. `FileType` fires the
moment vim decides what a buffer is, which is after the file is read and
after the shipped ftplugins have run — so an autocommand always wins
against an ftplugin, whether or not you meant it to. That ordering is the
single most common reason a setting "does not stick".

`textwidth=0` means never break. If you want the wrapping for prose but
not for the line you are typing right now, `:setlocal fo-=t` leaves the
option alone and takes away its licence to act on ordinary text — and
`gq` still reflows a paragraph on demand.

Autocommands are the next two lessons. This one is about finding them.

@start config.vim
" team defaults, inherited from the old tech lead
set ignorecase
autocmd FileType markdown setlocal textwidth=60
autocmd FileType gitcommit setlocal textwidth=72

@start CHANGELOG.md
# Changelog

## 2026.09.2

### Fixed

@expected CHANGELOG.md
# Changelog

## 2026.09.2

### Fixed
- Refunds outside the 30 day window now record a reason instead of failing.
